<%= render layout: "pages/legal_chrome", locals: {title: "Getting Started", updated: "30 August 2026"} do %>
  <section>
    <p class="text-grey">New to Hoojah? This short guide walks you through the four things you'll do most: posting a claim, voting on one, responding with your stance, and taking an argument to a debate.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Posting your first hujah</h2>
    <p class="text-grey">A hoojah is a claim you're prepared to defend. Tap the compose button, write your claim as a clear, single statement — the sharper and more specific it is, the better the argument it invites — and post it. Your hoojah appears in the feed, where others can immediately vote and respond. You don't need to argue your case in the post itself; the responses and any debate are where that happens.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Voting: agree, neutral, disagree</h2>
    <p class="text-grey">On any hoojah you'll see three vote buttons. Choose <strong>agree</strong> or <strong>disagree</strong> to take a side, or <strong>neutral</strong> if you've genuinely considered it and remain undecided — neutral is counted and displayed alongside the others, not treated as a non-answer. Voting is a secret ballot: only the running tallies are shown, and no one, not even the author, can see how you voted.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Writing stance-tagged responses</h2>
    <p class="text-grey">A vote registers your position; a response explains it. When you reply to a hoojah you pick a stance tag — agree, neutral, or disagree — so readers can see where your reply is coming from at a glance. Responses can themselves be responded to, building a threaded tree of argument beneath the original claim. Good responses give reasons, not just verdicts.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Starting a debate</h2>
    <p class="text-grey">When you want to settle a point one-on-one, challenge an argument to a debate. You become the challenger and invite an opponent; if they accept, the two of you take alternating turns through structured rounds — opening, counter, response, and closing. No one else can post inside the debate, but spectators watch and, when it concludes, cast a verdict for whoever they found more convincing. Debaters can agree to extend with extra rounds, and a debate left idle for more than seven days concludes automatically.</p>
  </section>
<% end %>
